      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Provocative tests for coronary spasm were performed in a group of 131 patients (124 men and 7 women) with recent (less than 6 weeks) transmural myocardial infarction. Coronary arteriography was performed 27 +/- 9 days after the onset of the infarction. The provocative test was performed using a single IV bolus of 0.4 mg of ergometrine. Aortic pressure, ECG and arteriograms of the two coronary vessels were repeated 3 and 5 min later. Provoked spasm was observed in 27 (21%) of the patients. In 13 (48%) the coronary spasm occurred in a vessel presumed to be responsible for the myocardial infarction, while it was observed in coronary artery unrelated to the area of the infarct in 14 (52% of the cases with spasm). Thus, this study demonstrates a high degree of reactivity of the coronary tree of patients with recent transmural myocardial infarction suggesting the likelihood of a role for spasm in the infarction process and offering some explanation for subsequent recurrent ischemic events."},
